[38;5;246m[48;5;237m❯ [38;5;231mWithout using any tools, write a 300-word poem about tides.[39m

[38;5;231m[49m⏺[39m Captain, here's the tides poem:

  [1mTwice a Day[0m

  The sea goes out the way a guest leaves,
  slowly, still talking at the door,
  taking its coat but not yet its hat,
  saying it really must be going.

  And the beach it leaves behind
  is a room after a party:
  ribbons of kelp across the floor,
  a crab hurrying sideways
  like someone late for the last train,
  small pools holding pieces of sky
  as if the sky had dropped its change.

  Children come with buckets.
  They kneel at the edges of the pools
  and find whole cities there:
  anemones opening like slow fireworks,
  a starfish holding on to a rock
  the way you'd hold a railing on stairs,
  a tiny fish that doesn't know yet

